    <!-- When To Refi Guide -->
    <div>
      <div class="rb-section" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
        <h2>When Does Refinancing Make Sense?</h2>
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:9px;overflow:hidden;font-size:.88rem;">
          <thead><tr style="background:#4f46e5;color:#fff;"><th style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:left;">Scenario</th><th style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:center;">Verdict</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;"><td style="padding:9px 13px;">Rate drops 1%+ and you stay 3+ years</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:center;color:#166534;font-weight:700;">&#10003; Strong yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:9px 13px;">Rate drops 0.5%&ndash;1% and you stay 2&ndash;3 years</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:center;color:#92400e;font-weight:700;">&#9888; Maybe &mdash; calculate it</td></tr>
            <tr style="background:#f8fafc;"><td style="padding:9px 13px;">You want to shorten from 30-yr to 15-yr</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:center;color:#166534;font-weight:700;">&#10003; Yes if cash flow allows</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:9px 13px;">You plan to sell in under 2 years</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:center;color:#9f1239;font-weight:700;">&#10005; Likely not worth it</td></tr>
            <tr style="background:#f8fafc;"><td style="padding:9px 13px;">Rate drops 0.25% or less</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:center;color:#9f1239;font-weight:700;">&#10005; Rarely worth it</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:9px 13px;">High-rate ARM resetting soon</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:center;color:#166534;font-weight:700;">&#10003; Likely yes &mdash; lock fixed</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="rb-section">
        <h2>No-Cost Refinance: Is It Worth It?</h2>
        <p>A no-cost refinance means zero out-of-pocket closing costs &mdash; but you pay via a higher rate (lender-paid) or by rolling costs into the loan balance. Key trade-offs:</p>
        <ul style="color:#475569;padding-left:1.4rem;line-height:1.8;font-size:.92rem;">
          <li><strong>Lender-paid (higher rate):</strong> No upfront cost; you pay slightly more per month forever. Good if selling soon.</li>
          <li><strong>Rolled-in costs:</strong> Closing costs added to balance; your payment is slightly higher; you pay interest on the costs. Good if cash-poor.</li>
          <li><strong>Standard refi:</strong> Out-of-pocket costs; lowest long-term cost. Best if staying 5+ years.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="rb-info">&#128161; <strong>Rule of Thumb:</strong> In a no-cost refi, you typically accept a rate 0.125%&ndash;0.25% higher than the standard refi rate in exchange for zero closing costs. If a lender offers 6.50% standard or 6.75% no-cost, you are paying $625/year extra (on $500k) in exchange for avoiding $12,000 in closing costs &mdash; break-even is 19 months.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
