  <!-- Content Sections -->
  <div class="rb-section">
    <h2>Rate-and-Term vs Cash-Out Refinance</h2>
    <p>There are two main types of mortgage refinance, each with a different financial calculus:</p>

    <h3>Rate-and-Term Refinance</h3>
    <p>Replaces your existing mortgage with one at a lower rate, different term, or both. Your loan balance stays approximately the same. This calculator is designed for rate-and-term scenarios. The primary goal is to reduce monthly payments and/or total interest paid over the life of the loan.</p>

    <h3>Cash-Out Refinance</h3>
    <p>Replaces your mortgage with a larger loan and you receive the difference in cash. If you owe $300,000 on a $500,000 home, you could refinance for $380,000, take $80,000 cash, and have a new $380,000 mortgage. Cash-out refis are commonly used for:</p>
    <ul style="color:#475569;padding-left:1.4rem;line-height:1.8;font-size:.95rem;">
      <li>Home improvements (kitchens, bathrooms, additions) that increase home value</li>
      <li>Debt consolidation &mdash; replacing high-interest credit card or personal loan debt with mortgage debt at a lower rate</li>
      <li>Investment property down payments or business capital</li>
      <li>Education or large planned expenses</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Note: Cash-out refis typically carry a slightly higher rate (0.125%&ndash;0.375%) than rate-and-term refis and require at least 20% remaining equity (80% LTV) to avoid PMI on the new loan.</p>

    <h2>How the 2026 Rate Environment Affects Refinance Decisions</h2>
    <p>In <%= currentYear %>, the refinance opportunity landscape is segmented by when borrowers originally took their loans:</p>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:9px;overflow:hidden;font-size:.88rem;margin:1rem 0;">
      <thead><tr style="background:#4f46e5;color:#fff;"><th style="padding:9px 13px;text-align:left;">Original Loan Year</th><th style="padding:9px 13px;">Original Rate</th><th style="padding:9px 13px;">Refinance Incentive</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;"><td style="padding:9px 13px;"><strong>2020&ndash;2021</strong></td><td style="padding:9px 13px;">2.5%&ndash;3.5%</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;color:#9f1239;">None &mdash; current rates are significantly higher; lock in and wait</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:9px 13px;"><strong>2022 (peak)</strong></td><td style="padding:9px 13px;">5.5%&ndash;7.0%</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;color:#92400e;">Minimal &mdash; rates are similar; wait for Fed cuts to widen gap</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:#f8fafc;"><td style="padding:9px 13px;"><strong>Late 2023</strong></td><td style="padding:9px 13px;">7.5%&ndash;8.0%</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;color:#166534;">Moderate &mdash; dropping to 6.5% saves $200&ndash;$400/month on $400k</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:9px 13px;"><strong>2024 (high rates)</strong></td><td style="padding:9px 13px;">7.0%&ndash;7.8%</td><td style="padding:9px 13px;color:#166534;">Growing &mdash; watch for 6.0%&ndash;6.5% window for break-even under 2 years</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="rb-info">&#128276; <strong>Rate Watch Tip:</strong> Set up a rate alert with your lender or on Bankrate/Freddie Mac's weekly survey. When 30-year rates drop 0.75%&ndash;1% from your existing rate, run this calculator to see if your break-even is under 24 months. If so, lock immediately &mdash; rates can move fast.</div>
  </div>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <div class="rb-section" id="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="rb-faq-item"><div class="rb-faq-q" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">When does it make sense to refinance?</div><div class="rb-faq-a">Refinancing generally makes sense when your new rate is at least 0.5%&ndash;1% lower, you plan to stay in the home longer than the break-even period (typically 18&ndash;36 months), and you have at least 20% equity. The break-even calculator tells you the exact number of months to recoup closing costs.</div></div>
    <div class="rb-faq-item"><div class="rb-faq-q" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">What are typical refinance closing costs?</div><div class="rb-faq-a">Refinance closing costs typically run 2%&ndash;5% of the loan amount. On a $400,000 balance, expect $8,000&ndash;$20,000. Common items include loan origination fee (0%&ndash;1%), appraisal ($500&ndash;$700), title insurance (lender policy required), and recording fees ($100&ndash;$300).</div></div>
    <div class="rb-faq-item"><div class="rb-faq-q" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">What is a no-cost refinance?</div><div class="rb-faq-a">A no-cost refinance means no out-of-pocket closing costs. You pay via a higher interest rate (lender-paid closing costs) or by rolling costs into the new loan balance. In a no-cost refi, the break-even point is day 1 since there are no upfront costs &mdash; but your rate is slightly higher.</div></div>
    <div class="rb-faq-item"><div class="rb-faq-q" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">Should I refinance to a 15-year or 30-year mortgage?</div><div class="rb-faq-a">A 15-year refinance offers a lower rate (typically 0.5%&ndash;0.75% below 30-year) and massive interest savings, but higher monthly payments. A 30-year refinance maximizes monthly cash flow improvement. On $400,000: 30-year at 6.75% = $2,594/month; 15-year at 6.00% = $3,376/month. The 15-year saves ~$235,000 in total interest but costs $782 more per month.</div></div>
    <div class="rb-faq-item"><div class="rb-faq-q" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">Does refinancing reset your amortization schedule?</div><div class="rb-faq-a">Yes. When you refinance, you start a new amortization schedule from the beginning. If you have 20 years remaining and refinance into a 30-year loan, you reset to 30 years. Even with a lower rate, this can mean more total interest over the extended period &mdash; choose a shorter term or make extra payments to counteract this effect.</div></div>
  </div>

